ur time-based number. Explain why the time-based number is the one you report, even if the two come out close."}, {"id": "M2", "text": "Try gradient boosting (LightGBM, XGBoost or scikit-learn) and compare it honestly with the baseline on the test window. Keep whichever wins, and show the five features that matter most."}, {"id": "S1", "text": "Churn rate by billing cycle, plan and payment method in the test window, with the population count behind each rate."}]}, {"key": "dash1", "title": "Phase 3a, Dashboard Page 1: Subscriber and Call List", "tasks": [{"id": "D1", "text": "Build a subscriber lookup: type a Customer_ID and see the profile, the monthly usage line, bills and tickets on one timeline, and the model's churn score at the test cutoff."}, {"id": "D5", "text": "Mark involuntary risk separately: subscribers whose last renewal failed at least once."}, {"id": "D2", "text": "Show the top reasons behind that score (SHAP values, or the features with the biggest effect)."}, {"id": "D4", "text": "Add filters for plan, cycle, payment method, language and tier that also drive the call list."}, {"id": "D3", "text": "Add a call list: the 1000 highest scored subscribers in the test window, with monthly revenue and the reason to call."}]}, {"key": "dash2", "title": "Phase 3b, Dashboard Page 2: Model and Money", "tasks": [{"id": "D7", "text": "A calibration chart: predicted against actual churn by score decile."}, {"id": "D6", "text": "Model page header: AUC, PR AUC, base rate and the lift in the top 10% on the test window."}, {"id": "D14", "text": "A threshold slider that updates precision, recall, calls made and rupees gained."}, {"id": "D10", "text": "The fade: average hours in the months before churn against stayers."}, {"id": "D15", "text": "Make every panel respect the segment filters."}, {"id": "D12", "text": "Feature importance for the final model, with leaky features shown greyed out as excluded."}, {"id": "D9", "text": "Churn rate by cycle, plan, payment method, language and tier, all in one view."}, {"id": "D8", "text": "A profit curve: rupees gained for every call list size from 0 to the whole population, using your offer cost and success rate, with your capacity marked."}, {"id": "D11", "text": "Voluntary against involuntary churn by month and payment method."}, {"id": "D13", "text": "A segment page for Monthly subscribers."}]}, {"key": "documentation", "title": "Phase 4, Documentation", "tasks": [{"id": "DOC1", "text": "Write a model card plus a business memo. The model card covers data, population, label, leakage you removed, features, validation and test results. The memo, for the head of growth, says how many people to call, who, and what it is worth in rupees with an offer of ₹129 saving 40% of churners, and what to do about involuntary churn, which a phone call will not fix."}]}, {"key": "stretch", "title": "Stretch ideas (optional)", "tasks": [{"id": "X-S6", "text": "Check your model for fairness across tiers and languages: precision and recall by group."}, {"id": "X-S3", "text": "Is the FIRST99 promo worth it? Compare 12 month revenue per subscriber with and without it."}, {"id": "X-S2", "text": "Estimate how much revenue would be saved by retrying failed renewals on day 1, 3 and 7 instead of only day 3."}]}], "brief": "**Your business brief (shared with 4 other buyers):** segment **Monthly subscribers**, offer cost **₹129**, save rate **40%**, call capacity **1000**.", "premium": [{"id": "X1", "text": "**Uplift, not churn.** Explain why the best people to call are not the highest risk ones, and propose how you would run a test to learn who responds to the offer."}, {"id": "X2", "text": "**Time-based validation done properly.** Add a third, earlier window (cutoff 1 July 2025) and show how stable AUC and feature importance are across the three."}, {"id": "X3", "text": "**Optimise the call list.** With your offer cost, success rate and capacity, choose the list that maximises expected rupees, and show the rupee difference against simply calling the top 1000 by score."}, {"id": "X4", "text": "**Calibrate it.** Calibrate the scores (isotonic or Platt), show the before and after, and explain why the profit calculation needs calibrated probabilities."}, {"id": "X5", "text": "**Ship it.** Write the scoring job as a function that takes any cutoff date and returns the call list, with tests that fail if a leaky column ever sneaks back in."}]}$pack$::jsonb
