e and involuntary share in the test window, and the monthly revenue those churners took with them."}, {"id": "M3", "text": "Train the same model on a random 80/20 split of the test window and compare its AUC with your time-based number. Explain why the time-based number is the one you report, even if the two come out close."}, {"id": "S5", "text": "Churn rate by language and tier. Which languages lose subscribers fastest, and is it about the catalogue?"}]}, {"key": "dash1", "title": "Phase 3a, Dashboard Page 1: Subscriber and Call List", "tasks": [{"id": "D5", "text": "Mark involuntary risk separately: subscribers whose last renewal failed at least once."}, {"id": "D4", "text": "Add filters for plan, cycle, payment method, language and tier that also drive the call list."}, {"id": "D2", "text": "Show the top reasons behind that score (SHAP values, or the features with the biggest effect)."}, {"id": "D3", "text": "Add a call list: the 1500 highest scored subscribers in the test window, with monthly revenue and the reason to call."}, {"id": "D1", "text": "Build a subscriber lookup: type a Customer_ID and see the profile, the monthly usage line, bills and tickets on one timeline, and the model's churn score at the test cutoff."}]}, {"key": "dash2", "title": "Phase 3b, Dashboard Page 2: Model and Money", "tasks": [{"id": "D12", "text": "Feature importance for the final model, with leaky features shown greyed out as excluded."}, {"id": "D8", "text": "A profit curve: rupees gained for every call list size from 0 to the whole population, using your offer cost and success rate, with your capacity marked."}, {"id": "D11", "text": "Voluntary against involuntary churn by month and payment method."}, {"id": "D14", "text": "A threshold slider that updates precision, recall, calls made and rupees gained."}, {"id": "D10", "text": "The fade: average hours in the months before churn against stayers."}, {"id": "D7", "text": "A calibration chart: predicted against actual churn by score decile."}, {"id": "D13", "text": "A segment page for Tier 3 cities."}, {"id": "D15", "text": "Make every panel respect the segment filters."}, {"id": "D9", "text": "Churn rate by cycle, plan, payment method, language and tier, all in one view."}, {"id": "D6", "text": "Model page header: AUC, PR AUC, base rate and the lift in the top 10% on the test window."}]}, {"key": "documentation", "title": "Phase 4, Documentation", "tasks": [{"id": "DOC1", "text": "Write a model card plus a business memo. The model card covers data, population, label, leakage you removed, features, validation and test results. The memo, for the head of growth, says how many people to call, who, and what it is worth in rupees with an offer of ₹99 saving 35% of churners, and what to do about involuntary churn, which a phone call will not fix."}]}, {"key": "stretch", "title": "Stretch ideas (optional)", "tasks": [{"id": "X-S4", "text": "Survival analysis: Kaplan-Meier curves by billing cycle and channel."}, {"id": "X-S6", "text": "Check your model for fairness across tiers and languages: precision and recall by group."}, {"id": "X-S1", "text": "Build a separate model for involuntary churn using only payment history, and compare it with the main model on those churners."}]}], "brief": "**Your business brief (shared with 4 other buyers):** segment **Tier 3 cities**, offer cost **₹99**, save rate **35%**, call capacity **1500**."}$pack$::jsonb
