": "A calibration chart: predicted against actual churn by score decile."}, {"id": "D14", "text": "A threshold slider that updates precision, recall, calls made and rupees gained."}, {"id": "D10", "text": "The fade: average hours in the months before churn against stayers."}, {"id": "D11", "text": "Voluntary against involuntary churn by month and payment method."}]}, {"key": "documentation", "title": "Phase 4, Documentation", "tasks": [{"id": "DOC1", "text": "Write a model card plus a business memo. The model card covers data, population, label, leakage you removed, features, validation and test results. The memo, for the head of growth, says how many people to call, who, and what it is worth in rupees with an offer of ₹79 saving 30% of churners, and what to do about involuntary churn, which a phone call will not fix."}]}, {"key": "stretch", "title": "Stretch ideas (optional)", "tasks": [{"id": "X-S4", "text": "Survival analysis: Kaplan-Meier curves by billing cycle and channel."}, {"id": "X-S6", "text": "Check your model for fairness across tiers and languages: precision and recall by group."}, {"id": "X-S2", "text": "Estimate how much revenue would be saved by retrying failed renewals on day 1, 3 and 7 instead of only day 3."}]}], "brief": "**Your business brief (shared with 4 other buyers):** segment **Telugu speakers**, offer cost **₹79**, save rate **30%**, call capacity **1200**.", "premium": [{"id": "X2", "text": "**Time-based validation done properly.** Add a third, earlier window (cutoff 1 July 2025) and show how stable AUC and feature importance are across the three."}, {"id": "X4", "text": "**Calibrate it.** Calibrate the scores (isotonic or Platt), show the before and after, and explain why the profit calculation needs calibrated probabilities."}, {"id": "X5", "text": "**Ship it.** Write the scoring job as a function that takes any cutoff date and returns the call list, with tests that fail if a leaky column ever sneaks back in."}, {"id": "X3", "text": "**Optimise the call list.** With your offer cost, success rate and capacity, choose the list that maximises expected rupees, and show the rupee difference against simply calling the top 1200 by score."}, {"id": "X1", "text": "**Uplift, not churn.** Explain why the best people to call are not the highest risk ones, and propose how you would run a test to learn who responds to the offer."}]}$pack$::jsonb
